[Music]  across the whole food system 20% of  fruits and vegetables that are grown  never make it to human mouth food waste  is a complex problem some of it is  because of the really tight aesthetic  standards of the grocery stores need to  be perfectly round same size the same  color my name is Ben Chester and I'm one  of the co-founders and imperfect foods  [Music]  imperfect foods is a grocery delivery  service that takes produce and groceries  that would normally go to waste and  delivers them to your door for 30% less  in the grocery store we started this  because we noticed there was an  environmental need and there is food  gonna waste a few toys for a country it  would actually be the third largest  emitter of greenhouse gases and then  there's all of the resources all the  water all the labor that goes to waste  we know the more people you reach bigger  impact were able to have in the  environment we wanted to be close to the  farmers and close to the source at over  half of the produce consumed in the US  has grown in California San Francisco  was we came out here with basically a  forklift a warehouse and the shirts on  our backs  the way we work with farms were here to  help them reduce waste so every week  farms basically send us a list of the  fruits and vegetables that they can sell  and we're putting together a complete  menu when you talk to the farmers you  really understand these fruits and  vegetables are kind of their children  and they put so much care and love into  growing them and they have so much pride  in their craft  I love farming because I like the  challenge I like always going up against  mother nature Lakeside organic Gardens  we farm over 3,000 acres we grow over 52  different types of vegetables cilantro  Farsi broccoli cauliflower parsnips  rutabagas turnips lettuces all types  romaine Greenleaf redly iceberg chard we  consider ourselves our one-stop shop for  organic vegetables  we're in our red cabbage field where  it's just been harvested here on my left  I have up a lakeside regular box and to  my right I have the imperfect box  all of them are uniform standard pretty  this just exactly the same same field  same deal just a little bit smaller  ideally for any farmer in this world the  perfect scenario is harvest every piece  of produce that's out in the field  delicious before we we teamed up with  imperfect foods you'd have quite a bit  of product left out in the field now we  have an outlet for those vegetables that  aren't as pretty but they taste just the  same as anything else like they say  don't judge a book by it's cover  same with vegetables you know if they're  a little bit off don't knock it till you  try it  we're here in the imperfect foods  warehouse in San Francisco and so this  is where all the produce the groceries  the meat the dairy everything comes in  from the farms so let's go take a look  at some of the product why would these  mandarins go to a perfect not a grocery  store you can see some of this like  external scarring roughness on the  outside or discoloration but a bit of  peeling the grocery stores really don't  want them and they would normally go to  waste on the farm but a year ago we  expanded beyond produce because we  noticed that there was food go to waste  across the whole food system it's really  well line every box of produce and  groceries we sell helps us fight climate  change and do something good for the  environment  one of the main things that drives us to  be better and to keep pushing is knowing  that our work is helping to leave the  planet in a better place than we found  it for our children now we're headed to  food shift which is one of our community  partners in the Bay Area so sometimes  then we have product that we can't sell  we wanted to still find a home so we  donate to organizations like food shift  we are a combination of food recovery  organization and a social enterprise  kitchen we educate folks who would  otherwise face high barrier to entry for  employment and we hire them for our own  enterprise catering and distribution of  produced meals I want everybody to be  mindful and be kind to each other  and food is a great way to do that  because when we break our proverbial  bread together I think we're closer we  think about our sameness we solve things  together  we put aside our differences so we even  embrace our differences what a privilege  that we get to eat and what a privilege  that we get to cook amazing that food is  really so central to people's identity  and it can really serve as a force to  bring people together and it's also one  of the biggest issues affecting climate  change and health and nutrition and  obesity and so it is really crazy how  this small seed turns a new piece of  fruit or vegetable and then basically  can have so many positive effects on the  world  [Music]

